The wheelwright, a sturdy man named Elias, was known throughout the village for his exceptional craftsmanship. His workshop, a modest structure filled with the rich aroma of wood shavings and the occasional whiff of linseed oil, was his sanctuary. The rhythmic sounds of his tools—hammers striking, chisels carving, and the gentle whir of the lathe—were the soundtrack to his days.

Elias had learned the trade from his father, who in turn had learned it from his. The lineage of wheelwrights in their family was both a legacy and a responsibility. He took pride in creating wheels that were not just functional but works of art. Each spoke was meticulously measured and carved, ensuring balance and strength. The hubs were crafted with precision, and the rims were shaped to perfection, ready to be fitted with iron tires that would withstand the harshest of terrains.

